# Working data that must never be committed:
# - article PDFs are copyrighted (corpus PDFs live in C:/temp/journals)
# - the Carlisle spreadsheets are working data, not repository content
corpus/**/*.pdf
corpus/*.pdf
corpus/ExpectedResults.xlsx
corpus/ActualResults.xlsx
TestPapers/
*Carlisle*.xlsx
One Sheet*.xlsx
rsconnect/
*.Rcheck/
*.tar.gz
.Rproj.user/
# R writes .Rhistory into whatever directory a session ran in
.Rhistory
IntegrityAnalysis.Rcheck/
IntegrityAnalysis_*.tar.gz
.NewCarlisle/
# the Boldt and Fujii working dirs (resolved PMIDs, manifests, PDFs)
.Boldt/
.Fujii/
tests/testthat/_problems/

# Security screen reports (tools/securityScreen.ps1).
#
# This repository is PUBLIC. A screen report describes findings BEFORE
# they are fixed, so committing one would publish an unfixed
# vulnerability with a map to it. The reports stay local; what gets
# committed is the adjudication AFTER the fix lands - the code change,
# its tripwire assertion, and a line in docs/security-screens/log.md.
# The screen log names local files and diffs, so it stays local too.
docs/security-screens/screen-*.md
tools/securityScreen.log

# Secrets that must never be committed, even by accident.
#
# .Renviron holds the ANTHROPIC_API_KEY the corpus adjudication uses.
# It is normally in the home directory, not here - but it was copied to
# three compute nodes on 2026-08-30, and a stray copy landing in the
# repository root is precisely the kind of accident that gets a key
# published. The salt and the crosswalk are already covered by the
# .NewCarlisle exclusion above; this closes the remaining hole.
.Renviron
.Renviron.*
*.pem
*.key
id_ed25519*
id_rsa*

# Python bytecode from the TATR run-along tooling
__pycache__/
*.pyc
tests/testthat/testthat-problems.rds
